14:13:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Here are the best mid-range neighborhoods for each stop: **Hanoi** - **Old Quarter** — Most popular for tourists; walkable to everything, tons of restaurants/cafes, can be noisy at night. Good mid-range options here run $30-50/night. - **Hoan Kiem Lake area (just south/west of Old Quarter)** — Slightly quieter than deep Old Quarter, still very central, a good middle ground if you want easy access without the late-night noise. - Avoid staying too far west (near West Lake) unless you want a quieter, more residential/expat vibe — beautiful area but less convenient for a short stay focused on food/culture in the old city. **Hoi An** - **Old Town edge / Cam Pho ward** — Walkable to the lantern-lit streets and river, but set back enough to avoid the densest tourist crush right at your doorstep. - **An Hoi Peninsula** (across the river from Old Town) — Good mid-range boutique hotels, still a short walk/bike ride to everything, slightly better value than staying directly in Old Town. - **Near An Bang Beach** — If you want to prioritize beach relaxation, staying near An Bang and taxiing/biking into Old Town (10-15 min) is a nice alternative — quieter, beachy, still close enough. **Hue** - **Near the Perfume River, close to the Citadel** — Puts you walking distance from the main sites; Hue is compact enough that almost any central hotel works well. - **Le Loi Street area** — Popular mid-range hotel strip, close to the river and a short ride to the Citadel and tombs. **Ho Chi Minh City** - **District 1 (Dong Khoi area)** — The main central district, walkable to Ben Thanh Market, Reunification Palace, War Remnants Museum; more polished/upscale mid-range hotels here. - **District 1 (Bui Vien area)** — Cheaper, livelier, backpacker-adjacent — good if you want budget-friendly energy, but noisier and the area to be most alert in at night, as discussed. - **District 3** — A quieter, more local alternative just outside District 1, still an easy 10-15 min ride/walk to the main sites, often better value for similar quality. **General booking tip:** Booking.com and Agoda tend to have better mid-range hotel inventory in Vietnam than Airbnb, and mid-range hotels here often include breakfast and are surprisingly high quality for the price (rooftop pools, etc., even at $40-60/night). Want me to also sketch a rough day-by-day budget (hotels, food, transport, activities) so you have a full cost picture before booking?
