14:14:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Here's how to handle money flow throughout the trip: **Before You Go** - Notify your bank/card issuer of travel dates to avoid fraud holds. - Bring one primary debit card (for ATM withdrawals) + one credit card (for hotels, bigger purchases, backup) + a small amount of USD cash ($100-200) as an emergency buffer — not for daily spending, just in case. - Skip pre-ordering Vietnamese dong from your home bank — exchange rates are worse than what you'll get in-country. **Currency Exchange** - Vietnamese dong (VND) is the only currency accepted for everyday spending — USD occasionally works at some tourist-facing hotels/tours but expect a bad exchange rate if you pay that way. - Best exchange method: **withdraw from ATMs directly in dong** rather than exchanging cash — better rates, more convenient. - If you do want to exchange physical cash, gold/jewelry shops often give better rates than official currency exchange counters or banks — this is a normal, legitimate practice in Vietnam, not sketchy. **ATM Strategy** - Use ATMs attached to major banks (Vietcombank, Techcombank, BIDV, ANZ) — inside bank branches or malls when possible, not standalone street ATMs late at night. - Vietnamese ATMs often have low withdrawal limits per transaction (around 2,000,000-3,000,000 VND, roughly $80-120), and may charge a fee (~$2-4 per withdrawal on top of whatever your home bank charges). - Check if your home bank has fee-free international ATM partnerships (Charles Schwab checking, some credit unions) — this alone can save you $20-40 over the trip. - Withdraw every 3-4 days rather than daily to minimize per-transaction fees, but don't carry more than ~$150-200 equivalent in cash at once for safety. **Daily Cash Flow** - Carry only what you need for the day (roughly $20-40 equivalent in dong) in your wallet/pocket. - Keep a slightly larger reserve (another $50-100 equivalent) in your money belt or hotel safe, topped up every few days from ATM runs. - Split cash across two locations when out (some in wallet, some in a zipped inner pocket or bag) so a single loss/theft doesn't wipe you out. **Card Use** - Credit card works fine at mid-range hotels, some restaurants in bigger cities, and malls — but street food, markets, and smaller local restaurants are cash-only. - Always have enough cash on hand for a full day's expected spending (transport, meals, small entrance fees) since you can't count on card acceptance outside cities/malls. - Notify your card company about Vietnam-specific travel dates, and consider a no-foreign-transaction-fee credit card if you don't already have one (Chase Sapphire, Capital One Venture, etc. — many good options with no annual-fee versions too). **Denomination note** - VND bills go from 10,000 to 500,000 — the number of zeros can be genuinely confusing at first (500,000 VND ≈ $20). Take a day to mentally calibrate; a common trick is dropping the last 3 zeros and treating it like a rough price in thousands of dong = tens of cents. Want me to also put together a packing list tailored to the regions/climate you'll be hitting, or a rough day-by-day time schedule (wake time, activity blocks, etc.) for the trip?
