14:14:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Here's a packing list tailored to your route (Hanoi → Ninh Binh → Hoi An → Hue → HCMC), assuming you're traveling in the **Feb-April window** we discussed as ideal for hitting the whole country. If you've landed on different dates, let me know and I'll adjust (especially for Hanoi, which gets notably cooler Nov-Feb). **Clothing** - 5-6 lightweight, breathable tops (linen/cotton blends handle humidity best) - 2-3 pairs of lightweight pants/trousers (useful for temple visits requiring shoulders/knees covered) - 2-3 pairs of shorts (for Hoi An/HCMC heat and beach time) - 1 light sweater or long-sleeve layer (Hanoi mornings/evenings can be cool, especially Feb-Mar) - 1 light rain jacket or packable poncho (brief downpours possible even in "dry" season) - Swimsuit (An Bang Beach, possible hotel pools) - Comfortable walking shoes (closed-toe, broken in already — lots of walking in Old Quarters and temple complexes) - Sandals/flip-flops (beach, showers, casual evenings) - 1 modest outfit for temple/pagoda visits (covered shoulders and knees required at some sites — Citadel, pagodas, Cu Chi area shrines) - Light scarf or sarong (doubles as temple cover-up, sun protection, or beach towel) **Health & Toiletries** - Sunscreen (high SPF, reef-safe if swimming) — pricier and less reliable to find locally - Insect repellent (DEET-based, especially for Ninh Binh, Mekong Delta, and evening outdoor dining) - Basic first aid kit: rehydration salts, anti-diarrheal (Imodium), any prescription meds in original packaging with copies of prescriptions - Hand sanitizer / wet wipes (useful for street food stops, markets) - Personal toiletries (most mid-range hotels provide basics, but bring your own if you're picky) - Motion sickness tablets if you're prone to it (winding roads to Hue, boat trips) **Electronics** - Universal power adapter (Vietnam uses Type A/C/F sockets, 220V) - Portable charger/power bank (useful for long travel days, flights, boat trips) - Phone with offline maps downloaded (Maps.me or Google Maps offline areas) in case of spotty data - Consider a cheap secondary phone or older device for street use, per our earlier discussion, keeping your primary phone secured **Documents & Money** - Passport + printed visa approval letter (if using visa-on-arrival) or e-visa printout - Photocopies/photos of passport, visa, travel insurance stored separately from originals - Travel insurance details (physical or easily accessible digital copy) - Debit + credit cards (as discussed), small USD cash buffer - Crossbody or anti-theft daypack for daily use **Miscellaneous but Useful** - Reusable water bottle (many hotels/cafes offer refill stations; helps cut plastic waste) - Small daypack for day trips (Ninh Binh, Cu Chi, Mekong Delta) - Earplugs (Old Quarter Hanoi and Bui Vien HCMC can be loud at night) - A dry bag or ziplock bags for boat trips (Ninh Binh, Mekong Delta) to protect phone/electronics from splashes - Laundry bag (mid-range hotels often offer cheap laundry service, so you can pack lighter than you'd think for 10 days) **What NOT to overpack** - Heavy jackets/cold-weather gear (unless traveling Nov-Feb in the north) - Too many shoes — one good walking pair + sandals covers 95% of this itinerary - Excess cash or valuables you don't need daily (leave in hotel safe, as discussed) Want me to also put together a quick daily itinerary schedule (rough wake times, activity blocks, meal slots) so you have a sense of pacing once you land?
